-- title:
--   Two homeomorphisms of the line with no common fixed point in $[c,d]$ generate an element carrying $c$ beyond $d$
-- statement:
--   Let $f$ and $g$ be orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of $\mathbb{R}$ and let $c,d$ be real numbers. Suppose **no point of $[c,d]$ is fixed by both**: for every $t\in[c,d]$, at least one of $f(t)\neq t$, $g(t)\neq t$ holds. Then some element $z$ of the subgroup generated by $f$ and $g$ satisfies
--
--   $$z(c) > d.$$
--
--   The hypothesis is a *pointwise* disjunction, and that is what gives the statement its content: it is strictly weaker than requiring that $f$ move every point of $[c,d]$, or that $g$ do so. Each of $f$ and $g$ may have fixed points throughout the interval, provided they share none — and then neither generator alone suffices, so a genuinely mixed word is needed.
--
--   The reason is that if every element of $\langle f,g\rangle$ kept $c$ at or below $d$, the orbit of $c$ would be bounded, and its supremum — a point of $[c,d]$ — would be fixed by the whole group, since an order isomorphism of $\mathbb{R}$ preserves suprema of nonempty bounded sets.
--
--   The element $z$ is merely existent: no word-length bound, no normal form, no claim about which generators occur, and $z$ is permitted to be the identity (which is what settles the degenerate case $c>d$, where the interval is empty). Since the closure is taken as a subgroup, inverses of the generators are available.
--
--   **Formalization note.** No piecewise-linear or compact-support hypothesis is imposed, so this holds throughout $\mathrm{Homeo}_+(\mathbb{R})$; and the argument never uses that there are exactly two generators.
-- source:
--   M. G. Brin and C. C. Squier, Groups of piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the real line, Inventiones mathematicae 79 (1985), 485-498; Lemma (3.4) resp. (3.5) of Section 3. Note the paper's own remark that these two lemmas 'apply to arbitrary orientation-preserving homeomorphisms of R' -- they are stated here in that generality, with no piecewise-linear hypothesis.

